<hr class="div">
<div class="scroll">
  <p>A crested serpent eagle apparently has excellent aim when pooping, speaking from personal experience here. I've also been hissed at by banded kraits, been dive-bombed by the same family of nesting magpies every May through September, and once pulled a pearl stingray out of a river. In hindsight, that’s not really smart.
But to be honest, I don’t mind it at all.
I don't mind being pecked, scratched, bitten, or hissed at. I don’t mind thawing frozen mice and feeding the remaining white blob to Latte the ball python. Actually, I do mind that a little.</p>
  <p>It isn't glamorous, and honestly, a little revulsion is part of the experience. It’s a messy reality that can't be captured on a glowing screen with tidy p-values. This data is unpredictable; it has teeth and a bad attitude. But a dataset that fights back is one you have to take seriously. 
And maybe that's the point. The best science isn't always conducted under fluorescent lights with sterile gloves and controlled variables. Sometimes it smells like river mud. Every close call is a reminder that the natural world does not care about your hypothesis, it simply is. Wildlife biology isn't a career I reasoned my way into. In the future, I hope to contribute to animal behavior and conservation research that embraces the unpredictability of the living world rather than reducing it to numbers alone.
</p>
  <div class="quote">"People are so preoccupied with whether they could, they didn't stop to think if they should."</div>
</div>
