// 3 - Primary Research (Fire Ant Social Polymorphism) — page 5 & 6
{l:`
<div class="pg-tag">🔬 II · PRIMARY RESEARCH</div>
<h2 class="sec">Chemical basis of social polymorphism in <em>Solenopsis invicta</em></h2>
<hr class="div">
<div class="scroll">
  <p><strong>Abstract:</strong> Red imported fire ant colonies exist as monogyne (single queen) or polygyne (multiple queens). BB genotype workers normally execute extra queens, but when mixed with Bb workers (even 10-15% Bb), BB workers begin accepting multiple queens. This study investigates two candidate chemical signals: cuticular hydrocarbons and venom sac piperidines. Using controlled colony mixing, transfer assays, and GC-MS analysis, we test whether these compounds induce queen-acceptance behavior in BB workers.</p>
  <div class="quote">"A single molecule, broadcast in silence, ends a queen's reign — or begins a new one."</div>
  <h3 class="sub">Research questions & hypotheses</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q1:</strong> Does contact with live or dead Bb workers alter BB cuticular hydrocarbon profiles?</li>
    <li><strong>Q2:</strong> Which specific compounds differ between monogyne and polygyne CHC fingerprints?</li>
    <li><strong>Q3:</strong> Can venom sac alkaloid extracts alone trigger the same behavioral shift?</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Hypotheses:</strong> (1) Chemical mimicry – BB workers acquire Bb-like CHC profiles after contact. (2) Signal interference – venom alkaloids disrupt nestmate recognition. (3) Dual‑signal – CHCs provide identity, piperidines provide acceptance/rejection instruction.</p>
  <div class="bc-row">
    <div class="bc"><strong>🧬 Indirect Genetic Effects</strong>Minority genotypes (10-15% Bb) can reprogram colony social structure via chemical mediators — a striking example of IGEs.</div>
    <div class="bc r"><strong>🧪 Approach</strong>Microcolony assays ± physical contact, GC-MS profiling, venom sac dissection, and behavioral scoring.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="pg-foot"><span>II. PRIMARY RESEARCH</span><span>pg. 5</span></div>
